PIMID: a fixed block size in the guest's stat results (-st-blksize B)

QEMU user mode passes the host file system's preferred I/O block size
(st_blksize, stx_blksize) to the guest's stat, fstat, lstat, newfstatat and
statx. glibc sizes a FILE's buffer from it (the smaller of st_blksize and
8192 bytes), so the guest's standard output was buffered in 8192 bytes when
the run's log was on a parallel file system (4 MiB blocks) and in 4096 bytes
on a local disk, in a pipe or in tmpfs. The buffer size changes the work the
guest does before and around its writes, and with it the results of
multi-threaded and message-passing programs (the same binary on the same node:
51347, 51580 and 51295 cycles for a log on Lustre, on /tmp and on a terminal).

With -st-blksize B (or QEMU_ST_BLKSIZE=B) every one of those calls reports B.
B = 0 (the default) leaves QEMU unchanged.

diff --git a/linux-user/main.c b/linux-user/main.c
index d6d3c95..699eeb7 100644
--- a/linux-user/main.c
+++ b/linux-user/main.c
@@ -369,6 +369,18 @@ static void handle_arg_tsc_step(const char *arg)
     qemu_tsc_step = v;
 }
 
+static void handle_arg_st_blksize(const char *arg)
+{
+    /* PIMID patch 0006: a fixed block size in the guest's stat results */
+    char *end;
+    unsigned long long v = strtoull(arg, &end, 10);
+    if (*end || !*arg || v > 0x40000000ULL) {
+        fprintf(stderr, "qemu: -st-blksize expects a number from 0 to 1073741824\n");
+        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
+    }
+    qemu_st_blksize = v;
+}
+
